update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('5716c1d9-c32d-47a3-90b2-be88d637aae9'::uuid,'466e77c47999b5c3735e4666df44618d','This is the premise about storage: "most such documents are likely to be stored as encrypted text." Run the support test. Why does the author think biography will end? Partly because of this. A reason offered for the conclusion is evidence, not the conclusion. A also hardens "likely" into "will be."'),
('28dd4f0c-3a92-4e30-8dbb-d279c9f10308'::uuid,'212e13afe894a4d0f59bac205c4ec4c2','This matches the first sentence piece by piece. "widespread use of encryption software" carries over unchanged. "will bring the writing of biographical history virtually to an end" becomes "will cause the writing of biographical history to decline significantly or cease." An almost-end is a significant decline, and an actual end is ceasing, so B covers the author''s prediction without adding or dropping anything.

The move to repeat: once you have located the conclusion, put it in plainer words and look for the answer that says the same thing. On Main Conclusion, the correct answer is usually a paraphrase of one sentence, not a new idea.'),
('521a059e-21a5-453f-a0c6-7dd5511d7a25'::uuid,'48febcd85701c3bc0751a8bafd1d50ec','This is close to the "so" claim: without passwords, historians lose the most revealing material. That claim is supported by the encryption premise, and it in turn supports the first sentence. Ask what it is for. Historians losing access is the reason biography will end. A claim that supports something else in the paragraph is not the main conclusion.'),
('e44d8779-ecc1-47c9-9c5d-c39104e57583'::uuid,'34339b91a0086bf354f53546076fe23c','This is the last-claim trap. D restates the final sentence: the most interesting, revealing material "will be unavailable to historians unless they have the necessary passwords." It feels like the conclusion because it follows "so," and it is a conclusion, but only an intermediate one.

Run the support test in both directions. Why should we believe biography will end? Because historians will lose the best material without passwords. Why should we believe historians will lose it? Because the documents will be encrypted. The password claim sits in the middle of that chain. The first sentence is the only claim that is supported and supports nothing further, so it is the main conclusion.'),
('e07233f4-c780-4715-9e57-d3fe27d6d275'::uuid,'d38f323b0e21270711942411a6967edc','The stimulus calls these documents biographers'' "principal sources" and implies the most revealing material sits in them. E turns that into a ranking of all sources, which the author never states. Even as an implied idea, it would be background that explains why losing the documents matters. It supports the prediction; it is not the prediction.'),
('4944ceab-0422-4199-8e02-7edc0a8977b8'::uuid,'75ef81343ce2b61a035c96db6400b9ac','The argument never states this. It is an inference you might draw from the decay premise, but a Main Conclusion answer has to restate the claim the author argues for. A is also about the wrong subject: the author''s point is that new elements are being made today, not what became of the original ones.'),
('3d908710-dcdc-42cf-939a-8e8dee7b371f'::uuid,'3e76e66bd0673a615e2cac5229a8c545','This is the second sentence with the pronoun swapped out. "these elements" means radioactive elements, and "are clearly still being created in the universe today" becomes "are being created in the universe today." Dropping "clearly" and "still" does not change the claim.

Confirm with the support test. Everything else in the paragraph (the decay rate, the conditional, the abundance) answers one question: why believe new radioactive elements are being made? When a sentence after a pivot is backed by everything that follows, you have found the main conclusion.'),
('b85010c5-c908-4040-9055-86364ec27052'::uuid,'c4f8a6211e59a2f413ea5b694beff19b','This is the final sentence''s conditional, and the "So" in front of it is the bait. It is a conclusion, drawn from the premise that most radioactive elements decay within a few million years. But the argument does not stop there. The author pairs it with the abundance of radioactive elements to prove something else: new ones must be forming. A claim that is supported and then used as support is an intermediate conclusion. When the last sentence opens with "so," ask whether it is used to prove anything further before you pick it.'),
('04653179-3829-40e3-b207-5aa70c64a2de'::uuid,'9a285637d0c63d7eaa23bfda6f4bdc86','This is the opening concession. "may have been created" is a possibility the author grants and then sets aside with "even if this occurred." Nothing in the paragraph argues for it. A claim the author concedes and moves past is background, not the conclusion.'),
('2f20ea1f-0b17-4a68-9ec1-2468dfdee632'::uuid,'6bc820570dbf9e25667cbc88af77fd2e','This restates the third sentence: instability plus the decay timeline. It is the first link in the chain. Because the elements decay so fast, almost none would be left without new creation. The author offers it to prove something else, so it is support, not the point being supported.'),
('e56f5350-2a1e-4aca-9ec4-308cfb782355'::uuid,'c0e746352067e841ae6114c613bc32d9','The columnist talks about one problem: the current recession. A turns that into a rule for every problem, and "never" makes it absolute. One myopia example can show that some fixes work without knowing the cause. It cannot show that no problem ever requires finding the cause.'),
('5138630c-551a-4445-95a6-436d74a84024'::uuid,'dab9f81405de288e07cde074f7ab7cf3','B swaps necessary for sufficient. The columnist says knowing the cause is not essential: you can improve the economy without it. B says knowing the cause "would not necessarily enable people to find a solution": knowing it might not be enough. Those are different claims.

Test it. Imagine knowing the cause always led straight to a fix. B would be false, yet the columnist''s point would survive, because the economy could still be improved without that knowledge. When a conclusion says something is not required, check that the answer is not really about whether it is enough.'),
('854c2b29-c8ec-4ec8-a015-1ac078d817f6'::uuid,'af09c2e8aca50ebcbdb49755154167b0','This is the opening fact that sets up the debate. The columnist offers no reason to believe the question is hotly debated, and the rest of the paragraph does not try to prove it. A claim that nothing supports and that only introduces the topic is background, not the conclusion.'),
('65edb325-e3ca-45a2-90a4-eb12989fc3e3'::uuid,'d4439fe3dd8f4b7c485ac35a5a4c1e2f','This is the second sentence translated. The columnist says it is "a mistake" to assume that answering the cause question "is essential to improving the economy." D says the same thing with "need not": you can improve the economy without ascertaining the cause of the recession.

The move to repeat: when the conclusion rejects someone else''s view, restate it as the denial of that view (essential becomes not needed), then match. D also keeps the scope to the current recession, which is what keeps it from overreaching the way A does.'),
('55e1e833-d3f5-49cc-8d2b-e7fc9a5b94f5'::uuid,'1c2d24a7b5b927274128e9eca44a32ee','This is the evidence, introduced by "after all." The myopia example is the reason the columnist gives for the claim about the recession. Ask which claim it is offered to prove: that knowing a cause is not essential to a fix. Support for the conclusion cannot be the conclusion.'),
('cf2fe075-c1e3-42e6-8f4b-4e821fc3b083'::uuid,'0bb13d33f95b5691b87cff50b7863203','This is the third sentence nearly word for word. "we can expect the volume of freight shipped by rail to grow" becomes "can be expected to increase." Confirm with the support test. Lower rail costs and flat trucking costs are both reasons to expect more freight on rail, and the freight claim is not used to prove anything else. When a claim is supported from both sides and supports nothing further, it is the main conclusion, wherever it sits.'),
('d0d30399-3d29-4f96-84e2-59ba63457555'::uuid,'e4adcba584a3823c7ed6bcf9dd0f7c22','This links the first two sentences: automation means fewer workers. The stimulus treats that link as a starting point and uses the worker reduction to explain the lower operating costs. Ask what it is for: it feeds the cost claim, which feeds the freight prediction. A claim that only feeds the chain is not the conclusion.'),
('b514a500-23ee-4d83-84a5-b9d0fb8c3107'::uuid,'e6397f40bb8c4db42e6e7e507a956ac4','This is the last sentence, and it is a premise. The author uses it to show that rail''s cost advantage is real: trucks are not getting cheaper too. It gives a reason to expect rail freight to grow, so it supports the conclusion rather than being it. Don''t pick the final claim just because it comes last.'),
('ccdf2112-537e-4d49-9ced-78b722afd0cb'::uuid,'c3eea82bba0a3cec0f7cab16d9302350','This is the strongest decoy because it is supported. "Since fewer railroad workers are needed, operating costs have been reduced" gives a reason for the cost claim, so the cost claim is a conclusion of sorts. But the author does not stop there. "This means that" takes the lower costs and draws a further claim from them: freight volume will grow. The cost reduction is supported and then used as support, which makes it an intermediate conclusion. The main conclusion is the claim at the end of that chain.'),
('3ec91f10-a31b-49e6-8a0c-3af94ac8047f'::uuid,'56b2a12c3df125494b971e7f11d2d074','This is background from the last sentence. It names the competitor so the cost comparison means something. Nothing in the paragraph argues that trucking is rail''s chief competitor; the author states it and uses it. An unsupported fact that feeds the argument is a premise, not the conclusion.'),
('26eb1366-0dea-4184-80d8-2ddf3efd4771'::uuid,'c644fb6c2aa0f56e65fe79f00099f4c7','The climatologist states the 4-degree warming as a fact and does not argue for it. A also changes the claim: the stimulus never says some scientists found this evidence, and it never calls the warming "significant." The warming is the starting fact; the argument is about what explains it.'),
('d1376c36-dca3-42ba-a7f3-670317b442a6'::uuid,'00a78641447b9ab096c6ea7229ee61b9','B is tempting because it points the same direction as the climatologist. But B says the warming is not a symptom of global warming. The climatologist only says that conclusion is "far from justified." Not proven is weaker than false. Natural cycles show the warming might be natural; they do not show it is.

Test it: could the climatologist accept that the warming really is caused by pollution, while still holding that the scientists have not yet justified saying so? Yes. So B overstates the conclusion. On Main Conclusion, match the author''s strength, not just the author''s side.'),
('f75897ef-f6fb-446e-be86-c99cf5167d1e'::uuid,'ff962f97296f6cd28f10f766e2fe8352','This is the rebuttal sentence with the pronoun filled in. "this conclusion" refers to the scientists'' claim that the warming is a symptom of global warming caused by human-generated air pollution. C spells that out and keeps "is far from justified." Everything else in the paragraph serves that judgment: the warming is the scientists'' starting fact, and the natural cycles are the climatologist''s reason.

The move to repeat: when a conclusion contains "this" or "that," fill in what it points to, then look for the answer that keeps the author''s exact strength. Here that strength is "not justified," not "false."'),
('9d8a7224-bd06-422c-bf8f-8184d4145a57'::uuid,'cc322d0f723eea574a435dfa840fffe3','D says what the natural-cycle evidence suggests: the warming might be natural. The climatologist never says this outright, and even as an implied step it plays a supporting role. It is why the pollution explanation is far from justified. Support for the conclusion is not the conclusion.'),
('55575f40-2926-4e36-8394-972cb728c7f8'::uuid,'0382cc81423cafeeded9f785d4a0aa20','This conditional never appears in the stimulus. It also adds a claim the climatologist does not need: that a natural cycle and human-caused global warming could not both be at work. The climatologist''s point is about the evidence, that the pollution explanation is not justified, not a rule about which causes exclude each other.'),
('5f94de0b-c9e8-424d-ab16-ebba4e5e1658'::uuid,'2a8d84f28861239162d6faaca85b70ae','Rossi''s first sentence is a general rule: it is undemocratic for anyone to live under a government that doesn''t represent their interests. Rossi then applies it to one group. Children''s interests sometimes differ from their parents'', so, by the rule, children need their own representation, which means the vote.

"Appeal to a general principle" is how the LSAT describes this move. When an argument opens with a broad "it is wrong / undemocratic / unfair to ..." claim and then applies it to a specific case, look for this answer.'),
('42536bcf-8259-468e-8e07-74eea53c32b6'::uuid,'fd3660b8b73891b09da92445d2dacdb4','Rossi speaks first, so there is no opponent yet, and Rossi never questions anyone''s honesty or motives. Smith doesn''t either: Smith grants Rossi''s point about interests and disagrees on the merits. An answer about bad faith needs an accusation in the text, and there is none.'),
('31f8e41a-ef98-4e07-ad56-220638f59925'::uuid,'cd914a250f83f2676f523f029694cb55','Rossi''s argument is about what is democratic, not about what would happen if children voted. Rossi never predicts a single result. Smith''s worry that children''s conceptions are too simple comes closer to practical concerns, which is why C can feel familiar: it fits the other speaker better.'),
('027271a5-c2dd-4155-9166-90f07b61e9bc'::uuid,'e938a05f7c7f717095ffc0501d2ac6d2','D can tempt because Rossi''s argument is short and sounds like a pair of statements. But Rossi does give a rationale. The policy is "children should have the right to vote." The rationale is the principle (living with unrepresented interests is undemocratic) plus the fact (children''s interests sometimes differ from their parents''). Test it: ask Rossi "why should children vote?" Rossi has an answer. An argument that substituted description for a rationale would only describe children or voting and never say why the policy follows.'),
('475f1a9c-a62a-4af2-869f-032a3e397a54'::uuid,'6f71131594dd69511b863c2b147aa3d2','This describes equivocation: one word used in two senses. The only term Rossi repeats is "interests," and it means the same thing both times: what is good for a person or group. With no shift in meaning, there is no equivocation.'),
('8ed8eaeb-3096-4036-9bc2-7f47c8dce089'::uuid,'9e112c269f919ba3b1f45200d73cad98','The ad never mentions customers, repeat or otherwise. Its only evidence is pride and winning. A Method answer has to describe what the argument actually does, and nothing in the ad demonstrates anything about who comes back to buy.'),
('b236d999-2cb4-48ed-9547-8f27b285fb86'::uuid,'8b4fb733688058dfb00ea9b0251c8a55','Check both halves. Conclusion: "superior to its competitors" matches "produce more winning automobiles than our competitors." Evidence: "using an analogy" matches the Austin Stables comparison. Pride made Austin beat other stables; Barr has similar pride; so Barr will beat other car makers.

When an argument borrows a result from one situation and claims it for a similar one, expect the Method answer to say analogy.'),
('7a1208fc-a4a5-494b-ba47-d72b07c207be'::uuid,'7fe6b3238a480fe33a2fce8f0c541b0f','C names something the ad does say: Barr has shown pride "For generations." But the ad only asserts it; nothing in the ad proves it. And it is a premise, not where the ad is going. The ad''s point is that Barr will out-win its competitors. Asserting a premise and proving a claim are different moves, and a Method answer has to describe the argument''s actual work.'),
('ed458bee-4c9e-4d4b-901d-9b7d0d73d625'::uuid,'ff98d25660921d578e9aaba606492c20','The ad credits pride fully: pride "accounts for" Austin''s success. It plays pride''s role up, not down. Understating it would undercut the ad''s own analogy, since Barr''s case rests on pride being what makes the difference.'),
('1ddd3869-909c-4414-80fa-6801deaaa9c4'::uuid,'88ebfedbe207c2002bacd0cb3b1ef4b5','The ad never compares the ages of the two traditions. It says Barr has shown pride "For generations" and calls Austin''s pride a tradition, but it never says which is older. The analogy needs the pride to be similar, not older.'),
('0f527ec1-f488-46a7-95c6-f2bb3a15c62f'::uuid,'a8fa0a1b65e248ad5927a1f1fed52a21','A is tempting because the evidence really does turn on a word with two readings: comprehension as grasping or as understanding. But check the X half. The author does not try to show the correlation is universal. The argument never disputes that it is "far from universal" and claims only that this is "insufficient reason to reject the researchers’ claim." A tendency can hold without being universal. An answer that gets the goal of the argument wrong fails even when its method half sounds right.'),
('ff966cbd-fa9f-4875-b058-d3043173a65b'::uuid,'eb7984380ceb2f4a41aa9de185603c6c','The fact the author cites is about "some people," not everyone, so it is not a universal generalization. And it is not offered as fresh support for a claim about gestures. It is offered to explain why certain exceptions do not count against the researchers'' claim.'),
('af40a8f1-dcb4-474f-a81e-6bfcef83793d'::uuid,'549223b4306ca11028116a884171e2b5','Match it piece by piece. "citing a psychological fact": some people perceive "comprehension" as a physical action. "a generalization": people tend to gesture less with abstract concepts. "apparently disconfirming evidence": cases where the correlation seems to fail. "reconcile": show those cases fit after all, because the speaker treats the concept as physical.

The move to repeat: when an author defends a rule against exceptions by explaining why the exceptions are not what they seem, the Method answer will talk about reconciling or explaining away the counterevidence.'),
('aa561e26-d14f-4d56-b28f-c8f0b5173efe'::uuid,'f1199372d99817f9f71bfa0a27317174','The author is not choosing among explanations for why people gesture. No rival explanation is named, and none is shown to be implausible. The argument defends the researchers'' claim against apparent exceptions, which is a different job from eliminating alternatives.'),
('6b8dc3a9-84b7-4b4d-91c3-9687a797ef2f'::uuid,'c49401092bbbc7ba0b9d9eaca0640544','E reverses the direction. The author argues the claim should not be rejected, not that it needs more support. E also calls the generalization "widely accepted," while the stimulus attributes it only to "Some researchers." The reason given is a reason the claim survives, not a reason to demand more evidence.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;